      {
        "narrative": "<p>Hustler Lake is in Ecological Lake Class 3, which consists of 72 lakes in northeast Minnesota that are average sized, deep, have very irregularly shaped shorelines, and have very clear and soft, infertile water.  Hustler Lake is very typical of these lakes.The water column in Hustler Lake stratifies in midsummer; there are 5 ppm oxygen to a depth of 25-50 ft at which depth the water temperature is 45-55 F, and there are 2 ppm oxygen to a depth near the bottom (74 ft) where the temperature is about 41 F.  There have been four previous biological surveys or fish population assessments on Hustler Lake, dating back to 1974.The total catch of fish in 1996 (all species combined) of 4.7 fish/gillnet was lower than normal for this lake class and lower than the long term average for this lake.  The total fish catch appears to have decreased over time, but this may be due to changes in netting locations, depths, and dates of the various surveys and assessments.   Lake Class 3 is one of the least biologically productive lake classes in northeast Minnesota.Fish populations in 1996, as in previous surveys and assessments, were dominated by northern pike and white sucker.Northern pike numbers in 1996 were normal for this lake class, but were lower than most previous assessment catches in this lake.  Pike sizes in 1996 averaged 21.5 in which is smaller than normal for this lake class, but similar to previous catches in this lake.  The largest pike caught in 1996 was 24.5 in (3.3 lb).  Most pike were age three.  Pike growth, 19.4 in at age three, was normal for the area.White sucker numbers in 1996 were normal for this lake class, but lower than in previous assessments.  Low numbers of bluegill and rock bass were captured in 1996, similar to previous assessments.  The cisco population in this lake may be substantial; cisco were caught in some previous assessments, but few of the gillnets in previous assessments and none of the gillnets in 1996 were in deep, cold water where cisco live.  Previous surveys and assessments captured yellow perch, pumpkinseed sunfish, and a variety of minnow and darter species. </p>",
